<div class="markdown-heading" dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" class="heading-element" dir="auto">Supported Languages</h3><a id="user-content-supported-languages" class="anchor" aria-label="Permalink: Supported Languages" href="#supported-languages"><svg data-component="Octicon" class="octicon octicon-link" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.042.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.751.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"></path></svg></a></div>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Traditional Chinese</strong> (繁體中文) — default when user writes in Chinese</li>
<li><strong>English</strong> — default when user writes in English</li>
<li>Bilingual abstracts (Chinese + English) for academic papers</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p dir="auto"><strong>Using a different language?</strong> Socratic mode (deep-research) and Plan mode (academic-paper) use <strong>intent-based activation</strong> — they detect the meaning of your request, not specific keywords. This means they work in <strong>any language</strong> without modification.</p>
<p dir="auto">However, the general <code>Trigger Keywords</code> section (which determines whether the skill is activated at all) still lists English and Traditional Chinese keywords. If you find the skill isn't activating reliably in your language, you can add your language's keywords to the <code>### Trigger Keywords</code> section in each <code>SKILL.md</code> file to improve matching confidence.</p>
</blockquote>
